  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThere will be just a few hours this morning where, with about 50kts \nin the critical layer, Coteau downslope conditions are favored. Flow \nabove this layer is unidirectional out of the west, so could be some \nlocalized 50 mph gusts on the southeast Sisseton hills/Buffalo ridge \narea.\n\nA weak wave crosses the region today, with an environment \nahead of this feature dominated by elevated skinny CAPE in a steep \nlapse rate environment with limited moisture. There is a warm nose \nbelow this instability, suggesting only the strongest elevated  \nconvection will result in moisture at the ground, though with the \nadded wrinkle of the potential for warm microburst type brief gusty \noutflows. \n\nThats about it for interesting weather. Surface high moves into the \nregion through the day. RRFS and GFS mixed winds top out around 25 \nto 30kts. Pressure rises only about 5 mb/6 hours.  We remain on the \neast side of the high tonight so gradient winds won't go slack \nkeeping temperatures from bottoming much cooler than 40F. Return \nflow for late Monday/Monday night. Warmest airmass is early Tuesday, \nwith 850mb temperatures close to +20C before passage of a cold \nfront. The wave associated with  this one is weak and moves through \nthe Western Lakes region, so should be dry for us. Northwest flow \ntransitions to ridging aloft thereafter, so conditions remain \nmid/dry for mid-week to late.",
